{
  "teamId": 118,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Kansas City Royals arrived as one of baseball's most pleasant surprises, a young and hungry team that fought their way back to October for the first time in nearly a decade. They stumbled out of the gates in March but quickly found their footing, posting back-to-back 17-11 months in April and May to establish themselves as genuine contenders in the AL Central. Salvador Perez provided veteran thunder, Bobby Witt Jr. announced himself as a star, and an unlikely cast of contributors kept the Royals afloat through the early months.\n\nJune brought turbulence — a losing month at 12-15 — but the Royals refused to fold. MJ Melendez launched clutch home runs, Maikel Garcia delivered timely doubles, and Bobby Witt Jr. continued to make jaw-dropping plays on both sides of the ball. The stretch was defined by the battle between big moments given up by Kansas City's bullpen and equally dramatic answers from their offense, a pattern that would define their entire summer.\n\nThe Royals steadied themselves in July and August, going a combined 28-23 over those two months. Witt's grand slam on July 29th captured the spirit of the team — resilient, explosive, unwilling to be put away. James McArthur's name appeared repeatedly in the wrong column, a reminder that the bullpen remained a work in progress, but the lineup kept answering. August saw the team weather a brutal single-day meltdown against Detroit and bounce back immediately, with MJ Melendez homering the very next day in a three-run blast to right field.\n\nSeptember faded on the Royals, a 11-14 record suggesting the tank running low, but they had done enough. Kansas City punched their ticket to the Wild Card round and dispatched the Orioles to reach the ALDS. There, the New York Yankees proved too much — Giancarlo Stanton and Gleyber Torres flexing October power — and the dream ended in the Division Series. Still, for a franchise that had been rebuilding for years, reaching the ALDS with 86 wins and a core of young stars pointed unmistakably toward a brighter future.",
